Algorithm Explanations
1. FCFS
Processes are executed in the order they arrive.

It is the simplest scheduling algorithm.

No sorting is required.

Advantages

Easy to implement.

No starvation.

Disadvantages

High waiting time.

Convoy effect may occur.

2. SJF
The process with the shortest burst time executes first.

Produces the minimum average waiting time (for non-preemptive scheduling when burst times are known).

Advantages

Low waiting time.

Better CPU utilisation.

Disadvantages

Burst time must be known in advance.

Long processes may starve.

3. Priority Scheduling
CPU is allocated based on process priority.

Lower priority number indicates higher priority in this program.

Advantages

Important processes finish earlier.

Suitable for real-time tasks.

Disadvantages

Low-priority processes may starve.

Aging is used to reduce starvation.

4. Round Robin
Each process gets a fixed Time Quantum (TQ).

If a process is not finished within its time quantum, it is moved to the end of the ready queue.

Advantages

Fair scheduling.

Good response time for interactive systems.

No starvation.

Disadvantages

Choosing an inappropriate time quantum can reduce performance.

Too small a time quantum increases context-switch overhead.

